course: dictionary
lesson: 14262
title: Newsworthy
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Newsworthy is an adjective. It describes something that is interesting or important enough to be reported in the news. «The school's new playground opened on Monday, but that is not newsworthy.» A playground opening is ordinary, so reporters would usually leave it out.
explain: Reporters ask: is this new, unusual, local, or important to many people? «A dog has just won a national race for the first time.» That is newsworthy because it is new and unusual. Use the same test on any event: if many people would want to read about it, it may be newsworthy.
explain: Newsworthy can change form: more newsworthy, most newsworthy. «The flood that closed the main bridge was the most newsworthy story this week.» To check your meaning, swap in "important enough to report" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Newsworthy means interesting or important enough to be reported in the news.
fact: Reporters decide what is newsworthy, often something new, unusual, local, or important to many people.
fact: «Newsworthy» is an adjective, so it can be «more newsworthy» or «most newsworthy».
fact: «The mayor's new park is newsworthy because many people will use it.»
fact: Something that happens every day, like a cat sleeping on a sofa, is usually not newsworthy.
exercise: «A dog has just won a national race for the first time.» Why is this newsworthy? | It happens every day | It is boring | It is about a cat | *It is new and unusual
exercise: Which of these would a reporter most likely call newsworthy? | A normal Tuesday with no changes | A cat sleeping on the sofa | A boy eating his breakfast | *A flood that closes the main bridge in town
exercise: Lina says, «Our bus was on time today, so it is newsworthy.» Is she right? | *No, an everyday event like this is not usually newsworthy | Yes, buses are always in the news | Yes, because it was a Tuesday | Yes, because she felt happy about it
test: «The new bridge will help thousands of people cross the river every day, so it is newsworthy.» What does newsworthy mean here? | *Interesting or important enough to be reported in the news | Very old and falling down | Quiet and hidden away
test: Which form finishes this sentence correctly? «This is the ___ story of the week.» | *most newsworthy | newsworthiest | more newsworthiest | newsworthyest
exam: «The museum's ancient coin was found in a field, and reporters agreed it was newsworthy because it was rare and important.» Why is the coin story newsworthy? | *It is rare and important | It is about a boring lesson | It is about the school dinner menu | It happens every day
